[job_20261010_a567a0ce] resolve.decision: Exactly on effective date: new amount: encoder_wrong. Clause 1 raises the monthly amount from Rs 1,500 to Rs 2,100, and clause 2 makes this apply from 1 April 2025. The rulebook still shows monthly_benefit = 1500 as of 2026-10-10, so the encoder's change was never applied. The likely cause is the encoder's ambiguity about the synthetic-document footer. That footer only labels the file as a test document. It does not change the content of the order, so it is not a real ambiguity. A woman who is eligible on 2025-04-01 should get Rs 2,100, because 'from 01 April 2025' includes that day.
[job_20261010_a567a0ce] resolve.decision: After effective date typical case: encoder_wrong. On 2025-06-10 the revision (clause 1, effective 1 April 2025 under clause 2) is in force. The woman meets every eligibility condition, so she should receive Rs 2,100. The rulebook returns Rs 1,500 because monthly_benefit was never updated.
[job_20261010_a567a0ce] resolve.decision: Other-scheme income Rs 1,000 after increase: top-up to 2,100: encoder_wrong. The rulebook pays max(0, monthly_benefit - other_scheme_monthly_inr). Because monthly_benefit is still 1500, it returns 500. Clause 1 makes the amount payable to each eligible woman Rs 2,100 from 1 April 2025. On that reading, a woman receiving Rs 1,000 elsewhere is topped up by 2100 - 1000 = 1100. The fix is the same one parameter, monthly_benefit. The top-up formula itself needs no change.
[job_20261010_a567a0ce] resolve.decision: Other-scheme income exactly 1,500 (unchanged limit) after increase: encoder_wrong. Clause 3 keeps the other eligibility rules unchanged. Under clause 5(4) of the original GR, 'more than Rs 1,500' is strict, so exactly Rs 1,500 from other schemes still qualifies. The rulebook correctly marks her eligible. Its payment of Rs 0 comes from the stale monthly_benefit = 1500 (1500 - 1500). With the new Rs 2,100 amount, the top-up is 2100 - 1500 = 600.
[job_20261010_a567a0ce] resolve.decision: Age 60 still eligible after increase: encoder_wrong. The age range of 21 to 60 is unchanged under clause 3, and the glossary treats age 60 as inside the range. The rulebook correctly keeps her eligible. She is paid Rs 1,500 instead of Rs 2,100 only because monthly_benefit was not updated for the date 2025-04-15.
[job_20261010_a567a0ce] resolve.decision: Age 21 (lower bound) eligible after increase: encoder_wrong. Age 21 is still inside the unchanged 21-to-60 range (clause 3), and the rulebook shows her eligible. She is paid Rs 1,500 instead of Rs 2,100 only because the new amount was never recorded.
[job_20261010_a567a0ce] resolve.decision: Income exactly Rs 250,000 eligible after increase: encoder_wrong. The income ceiling of Rs 2.5 lakh is unchanged (clause 3), and the glossary reads 'should not be more than' as inclusive. A family income of exactly Rs 250,000 therefore qualifies, and the rulebook marks her eligible. The payment is wrong only because monthly_benefit was not raised to Rs 2,100 from 1 April 2025.
